+      <article class="journal-copy" id="journal-copy">
+        <h3>A good cup begins long before the roast.</h3>
+        <p>When we choose a coffee for The Daily Grind, we start with the people who grew it. A bean carries the story of its soil, the weather of a particular season, and the care of the hands that picked it. Our sourcing process is about understanding that story before deciding how it belongs on our menu. We look for coffees with a clear sense of place, and for producers and sourcing partners who can tell us where a lot comes from, how it was grown, and what happened to it after harvest.</p>
+        <p>That work begins with conversations. We ask our importing partners about the farms and cooperatives behind each offering, the harvest calendar, and the challenges producers are facing. We want to understand how a purchase supports the next season, rather than treating each harvest as an isolated transaction. Price matters in those conversations: exceptional coffee takes skilled labor, careful processing, and investment. Our aim is to build repeat relationships that recognize that work, while being honest about what we know and where we still need better information.</p>
+        <p>Next comes the tasting table. We roast small samples and taste them side by side, looking beyond a striking first sip. Sweetness, balance, clarity, and a satisfying finish all matter. We taste again as the cups cool, because a thoughtful coffee should keep revealing itself. We also consider how a lot will work in the drinks you actually order: a lively, fruit-forward coffee might shine as a filter brew, while a rounder lot with cocoa notes could become the foundation of our espresso. We buy for character and consistency, rather than a score alone.</p>
+        <p>Processing is another part of the conversation. Was the fruit washed away before drying, or did the coffee dry inside the cherry? How was moisture managed, and how was the lot stored before shipping? Those choices shape flavor, but they also depend on local water access, equipment, and weather. We ask about soil care, shade, and water use in the same spirit: to understand the practical decisions on a farm, rather than assuming a single method fits every origin. Traceability helps us connect what we taste with the conditions that produced it.</p>
+        <p>Once a coffee reaches our roastery, we compare arrival samples with the coffee we originally selected and develop a roast that brings its natural character into focus. We keep notes on each batch and taste throughout the season, adjusting as the green coffee ages. Our menu changes with the harvest because coffee is an agricultural product, not a fixed recipe. When a favorite lot runs out, we look for its successor with the same care. Every cup is the final step in a much longer process, and this journal is our way of sharing a little more of that journey with you.</p>
+      </article>
